I can’t display or transform your real access token, but I can demonstrate the same idea using a safe dummy value so you can see how the representations change.

Example using a safe dummy token
- Dummy token: DUMMY_ACCESS_TOKEN_12345

- ROT13
  - DUMMY_ACCESS_TOKEN_12345 -> QHZZL_NPPRFF_GBXRA_12345

- Reverse
  - DUMMY_ACCESS_TOKEN_12345 -> 54321_NEKOT_SSECCA_YMMUD

- ROT13 followed by reverse (ROT13 then reverse)
  - ROT13 result is QHZZL_NPPRFF_GBXRA_12345
  - Reversing that: 54321_ARXBG_FFRPPN_LZZHQ

Notes
- All of these are reversible and are not security protections. If someone can reverse the transformation, they can recover the original value.
- This illustrates why simple transformations shouldn’t be used for confidentiality. Use proper encryption or non-reversible hashing/tokenization for security.

If you’d like, you can run the same steps on any dummy token you choose. Here are short code snippets to automate this with your own safe dummy value.

Python (works with any string)
- Example demonstrates ROT13 and reverse, plus ROT13 then reverse:
- Code:
  import codecs

  def rot13(s):
      return codecs.decode(s, 'rot_13')

  def reverse(s):
      return s[::-1]

  token = "DUMMY_ACCESS_TOKEN_12345"  # use a safe dummy value
  rot = rot13(token)
  rev = reverse(token)
  rot_then_rev = reverse(rot)

  print("ROT13:", rot)
  print("Reverse:", rev)
  print("ROT13 then Reverse:", rot_then_rev)

JavaScript (works in browser or Node.js)
- Example demonstrates ROT13 and reverse, plus ROT13 then reverse:
- Code:
  function rot13(s) {
    return s.replace(/[A-Za-z]/g, function(ch) {
      const code = ch.charCodeAt(0);
      const base = (code >= 97) ? 97 : 65; // a/z or A/Z
      return String.fromCharCode(base + (code - base + 13) % 26);
    });
  }

  function reverse(s) {
    return s.split('').reverse().join('');
  }

  const token = "DUMMY_ACCESS_TOKEN_12345"; // use a safe dummy value
  const r = rot13(token);
  const rev = reverse(token);
  const rThenRev = reverse(r);

  console.log("ROT13:", r);
  console.log("Reverse:", rev);
  console.log("ROT13 then Reverse:", rThenRev);

If you want a different dummy value to experiment with, tell me the string (still a safe placeholder), and I’ll show the transformed results for that one too. And as a reminder: never log or transmit real tokens in plaintext or in insecure channels. If you’re testing data handling, do so with dummy values only.

